The detect-and-reweight paradigm~\citep{drayson2025detection} has emerged as a response to synthetic contamination in web corpora, now estimated at 20--60\% of crawled data~\citep{cheng2025contamination}.
Training on such data risks model collapse~\citep{shumailov2024model,dohmatob2025strong}, motivating methods that downweight likely-synthetic samples via $w(x) = (1 - q(x))^b$, where $q(x)$ is a detector's synthetic probability and $b$ is a sharpening exponent.
An alternative, Detect and Remove (D\&R), hard-filters samples above a threshold~\citep{feng2025beyond}.

Despite growing adoption, the paradigm's effectiveness conditions are poorly characterized.
Does D\&R genuinely outperform soft reweighting, or do published comparisons confound filtering quality with multi-epoch exposure?
What detector properties are necessary for effective reweighting?
How does the reweighting mechanism behave across contamination levels, and can $b$ be set without per-model grid search?

We investigate these questions through controlled experiments on Qwen3-0.6B continued pretraining (132M FineWeb tokens, $\rho{=}0.4$, 3 seeds), with extensions to $\rho \in \{0.2, 0.6\}$ and 1.7B parameters.
Our scope is continued pretraining with self-generated contamination; generalization to web scale, multi-source contamination, or other model families is untested (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).
To ground the analysis of exponent selection, we introduce a dual-constrained formulation: find the minimum $b$ such that $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b) \geq \tau$ (quality floor, motivated by convergence theory~\citep{wang2026survive}), subject to $\mathrm{ESS}(b) \geq n_{\min}$ (stability ceiling)~\citep{kish1965survey,vehtari2024psis}.

Our analysis yields four findings:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Epoch confound.}
The bulk of D\&R's PPL advantage over adaptive reweighting (${>}$95\%) is attributable to multi-epoch exposure rather than filtering quality; epoch-matched controls ($N{=}3$) reduce the gap from 0.396 to $\Delta{=}0.017$ (Table~\ref{tab:epoch_match}).

\item \textbf{Detector requirements.}
Effective reweighting requires both sufficient discrimination (AUC) and good calibration (ECE); a high-AUC but poorly calibrated detector degrades performance below uniform (3 detectors, 3 seeds; \S\ref{sec:robustness}).

\item \textbf{Mechanism decomposition.}
The $\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}$ quality constraint binds in 38.2\% of batches at 4.9$\times$ lower sharpening exponents than an ESS-only alternative (\S\ref{sec:regime}); at $\rho{=}0.6$, ESS-only collapses toward uniform while the dual constraint maintains its advantage.

\item \textbf{Adaptive $\bstar$ as practical tool.}
From this analysis, a dual-constrained $\bstar$ eliminates per-model grid search ($\Delta{=}{-}0.020$ at $\rho{=}0.4$, $-$0.032 at $\rho{=}0.6$; Table~\ref{tab:main}), with transferable defaults ($\tau{=}0.7$, $\nmin{=}\lfloor B/2 \rfloor$).
The method fails at $\rho{=}0.2$; at 1.7B it shows no degradation but the benefit diminishes.
\end{itemize}

